  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Digital Media and Conflict Prevention</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&lt;p&gt;Brazil heads now towards an extremely sensitive political process, largely due to mounting&amp;nbsp;challenges that are converging to create a highly unstable situation not only for 2022, but for the years to come. Local manifestations of global patterns seen in other democratic countries represent growing threats to the institutions of democracy and human rights, especially in the form of political disruption, intensified by digital environments. The spread of hate speech, misand disinformation against women and minorities, online political violence and digital harassment, extreme political polarization, promotion of distrust in public institutions such as the electoral process or the justice system &#xbf; these are all trends intensified by digital media, potentially leading to conflict and degradation of democracy, the informational ecosystem and the space for civil society organizations and social movements. Recent studies have generated strong evidence on the threats posed by, for instance, fraudulent allegations against the electoral process, the threat of widespread hate speech in digital environments and the trend of fragmentation in social media, resulting in safe spaces for extreme political discourse.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Building on previous extensive experience, this project intends to develop a highly comprehensive approach to pursue the general objective of strengthening Brazilian democracy through the promotion of the integrity of informational space and digital environments.&lt;/p&gt;</narrative>
  </description>
